<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Enfield, London</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Enfield is a town in north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, now the centre of a London borough. From 1816 to 1988 it was the site of the Royal Small Arms Factory at Enfield Lock, which made the Lee-Enfield rifle and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bren-light-machine-gun">Bren light machine gun</a> for the British armed forces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/enfield-london.jpg" alt="Enfield, London" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christine Matthews · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Andrew%27s_Parish_Church,_Enfield_-_geograph.org.uk_-_187730.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Enfield lies south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hertfordshire">Hertfordshire</a> border and the M25 motorway, with Waltham Cross to the north, Edmonton and Winchmore Hill to the south, and Chingford and Waltham Abbey across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-lea">River Lea</a> to the east. The centre of Enfield Town is about 10 miles north of Charing Cross. The east, along the River Lea and the Lee Navigation, holds its industrial heritage, from the Royal Small Arms Factory at Enfield Lock to Wright's Flour Mill at Ponders End. Enfield Chase, a former royal hunting ground, lies to the west, and the New River runs through the town from north to south.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name probably comes from Old English Ēanafeld, open land belonging to a man called Ēana. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> times the manor was held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ansgar">Ansgar</a> the Staller, an officer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor">Edward the Confessor</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domesday-book">Domesday Book</a> of 1086 it belonged to Geoffrey de Mandeville. The church of St Andrew was given to Walden Abbey in Essex in 1136. In 1303 Edward I granted Humphrey de Bohun a weekly market and two yearly fairs, turning the village green into a marketplace. The market declined in the 18th century, revived in the 1870s and still runs; the September fair was suppressed in 1869 as a source of disorder.</p><p>The ancient parish of the Edmonton Hundred of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middlesex">Middlesex</a> was the largest in the county, 12,460 acres in 1831. Robert Uvedale, headmaster of Enfield Grammar School, kept some of the earliest hothouses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in the late 17th century, and the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-keats">John Keats</a> went to school in the town. Enfield was absorbed into the growing capital between the world wars. On 27 June 1967 the world's first cash machine, invented by John Shepherd-Barron, was installed at a branch of Barclays Bank in Enfield Town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Enfield became an urban district in 1894 and a municipal borough in 1955, and in 1965 it merged with Southgate and Edmonton to form the London Borough of Enfield, whose council sits at the Civic Centre in Silver Street. The town had a population of 156,858 in 2018. Forty Hall stands on the site of the Tudor Elsyng Palace, and St Andrew's Church keeps masonry of the 13th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Enfield, London” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
